ant to build  your starting strength  just use partial reps partial reps being  repetitions where you're in a use part  of the movement and of course in this  case you're going to train that initial  first part of the movement this is all  fairly obvious so far right you've got  explosive movements great for training  explosive movements makes a lot of sense  not particularly interesting what I find  more interesting and more unusual is the  use of eccentric training so eccentric  training is essentially performing  negatives in bodybuilding a negative is  where you lift a weight that's too heavy  for you or you get to failure to the  point where you can't lift away anymore  you use your other hand or spotter or  cheat rotor to get that weight back into  the upwards position and then you slowly  lower it so this is the concentric  portion of a movement it's where your  muscle shortens and the eccentric  portion or movement is where the muscle  lengthens usually that means you're  lowering the weight or lowering your own  body so the eccentric portion of the  movement is something that we often  ignore we curl the way up as quickly as  we can or we press the weight up as  quickly as you can  then we just let it gradually lower  using gravity gravity does the work for  us but that's a mistake because training  your eccentric portion of your movement  can actually build a lot more explosive  strength and it has a lot of other  benefits too so you're actually stronger  in the eccentric portion of the movement  turns out you're 1.75 times stronger  than you are in the concentric portion  so by emphasizing that eccentric side of  the exercise you're actually utilizing  your greatest strengths and then what's  interesting is that this appears to  correlate with increased explosiveness  and there are lots of good reasons for  that because if you think about it when  you explode and you jump or whether you  throw you normally kind of crank back  first say for example if you'